{
  "title": "Recursive AI Self-Improvement, Market Power, and Growth",
  "author": "Oliver Pardo\nDepartamento de Administración\nPontificia Universidad Javeriana\npardoo@javeriana.edu.co",
  "date": "September 2026",
  "abstract": "I develop an endogenous-growth model in which a monopolistic AI developer supplies AI services and invests in recursive AI self-improvement. I characterize two long-run regimes under an upper bound on AI efficiency—the quantity of AI services produced per unit of compute. Complementarity between AI and labor, or a sufficiently low upper bound on AI efficiency, can leave labor as a production bottleneck. In this regime, output per worker and wages eventually grow at the exogenous rate of labor-augmenting technological progress. When AI and labor are substitutes and the upper bound is sufficiently high, an AI-dominated regime sustains permanently faster wage growth and a higher long-run return to capital. Output per worker grows faster than wages, so labor’s income share vanishes while AI-industry revenue increases as a share of output. Replacing monopoly with perfect competition eliminates private research incentives, but sufficiently high initial efficiency sustains AI-dominated growth through capital accumulation and expanding AI use. Monopoly rewards research but restricts AI use: it can therefore produce more efficient AI yet slower long-run growth than competition. Numerical simulations show that early output-per-worker growth can remain moderate and similar across economies approaching different long-run regimes.",
